Certainly! Below is a simple C program that simulates rolling 6 dice using a custom random number generator. The program will print the result of each die roll.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

// Custom random number generator
int custom_rand(int min, int max) {
    return (rand() % (max - min + 1)) + min;
}

int main() {
    int dice[6];
    int i;

    // Seed the random number generator
    srand(time(NULL));

    // Roll the dice
    for (i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        dice[i] = custom_rand(1, 6);
    }

    // Print the results
    for (i = 0; i < 6; i++) {
        printf("Die %d: %d\n", i + 1, dice[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This program uses the `rand()` function from the C standard library to generate random numbers. The `custom_rand` function takes a minimum and maximum value and returns a random number within that range. The `srand()` function is used to seed the random number generator with the current time, ensuring different results each time the program runs.
